<section class="exhibits prism" aria-label="In this room">

  <div class="drawing-wall" id="why">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Where do your <b>fingers</b> go?</h2>
      <p class="wall-copy">When you want to learn music or play an instrument there’s a lot to absorb. Beyond the muscle mechanics of <em>how</em> to move your fingers, and fast enough, there’s the question of <em>where</em> to put them.</p>
      <p class="wall-copy soft">Most chord books give you every possible combination. Most software is too complex and offers too many choices. Neither is much use when the instrument is in your hands and you’re wondering, <em>how’s this work again…?</em></p>
      <p class="wall-copy">Music Prism is a focused, interactive reference that helps you grok how music works in practice. Change from major to minor and <em>see</em> how the notes move on the fretboard or keyboard. Oh, <em>that’s</em> how a maj7 works. What <em>is</em> a sus4, anyway?</p>
      <p class="wall-copy soft">If you’ve ever struggled to understand music while you were practicing it, keep Music Prism open next time.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="drawing">
      {% include "musicprism/hero-sheet.html" %}
    </div>
    <p class="caption"><b>Sixty chords, drawn on the wall</b> By Music Prism’s own diagram engine, the code that draws them in the app.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="seven" id="instruments">
    <div class="seven-head">
      <h2>The same chord, seven instruments</h2>
      <div class="copy">
        <p>Guitar, standard and baritone. Bass, four-string and five. Ukulele, standard and baritone. And piano. Learn one instrument, or learn how the notes and chords translate from one to the next.</p>
        <p>A chord on the fretboard and the same chord on the keyboard are two views of one thing: the diagrams all come from the same engine. Here, the same C chord, drawn for each of the seven instruments.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-instrument.html" %}
      </div>
    </div>
    {% include "musicprism/seven-instruments.html" %}
  </div>

  <div class="plates" id="plates">
    <h2 class="visually-hidden">What’s inside</h2>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>Inversions <span class="ing">=</span> Voicings</h3>
        <p>“Inversions” are the different ways one chord can be voiced: the same notes, stacked in another order, in another place. You can play a chord in a lot of spots on the fretboard or keyboard, and which one you reach for depends on where your hand is coming from and where it’s going next in the song.</p>
        <p>The standard advice is to learn them all, up and down the neck, in every key. Music Prism gives you a way to work through that methodically, and a reference to come back to as you grow.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-view-inversions.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("inversions-guitar", "Music Prism showing every inversion of a C7 chord on guitar: C7, C7/E, C7/G, C7/B♭, across each adjacent set of strings.") }}
          <figcaption><b>C7 on guitar</b>Every inversion, across every string set.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("inversions-piano", "The same C7 on the piano keyboard, its four inversions laid out as close-position voicings.") }}
          <figcaption><b>The same C7 on piano</b>Four inversions in close position.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>Chord qualities</h3>
        <p>How is major different from minor? How do you turn either one into a maj7 or an m7? What’s the diminished shape again? Would sus2 work here, or sus4?</p>
        <p>Switch the quality and watch the diagram change under you. Play the shapes on your instrument and let your mind start making the connections. That’s how a chord gets under your fingers.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-quality.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        {%- set qualities = [["major", 0], ["minor", 2], ["m7", 3], ["dim", 4], ["dom7", 5], ["sus2", 6], ["sus4", 7]] %}
        <figure class="exhibit">
          <div class="shot-cycle shot-cycle-8">
            {{ shot("quality-guitar-maj7", "Music Prism showing a Dmaj7 chord on guitar, redrawn through all eight qualities in turn as the Quality control cycles.") }}
            {% for q in qualities %}{{ frame("quality-guitar-" + q[0], q[1]) }}{% endfor %}
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>One D chord, eight qualities</b>On guitar, redrawn at each turn.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="exhibit">
          <div class="shot-cycle shot-cycle-8">
            {{ shot("quality-piano-maj7", "The same Dmaj7 on the piano keyboard, following the same cycle through all eight qualities.") }}
            {% for q in qualities %}{{ frame("quality-piano-" + q[0], q[1]) }}{% endfor %}
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>The same D on piano</b>Through the same eight.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>What <em>is</em> this chord?</h3>
        <p>You’re playing around and you find something you like. What <em>is</em> that? Click the notes on a blank fretboard or keyboard and the chord gets named from its intervals, along with any other reading the same notes allow.</p>
        <p>For the experts: the lowest sounding note decides between a plain name and a slash chord.</p>
        <p>When the chord turns up in one of the app’s progressions, one more click shows it there, in a key where it’s at home.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-view-identify.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("identify-complex", "Music Prism’s Identify view naming a grip marked on a blank fretboard, x x 7 7 5 5, as Asus4, with the alternate reading Dsus2/A.") }}
          <figcaption><b>Asus4, also known as Dsus2/A</b>You found it by ear at the seventh fret, and now you know what it is.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("identify-door", "Identify naming the grip x 7 9 8 9 x as Emaj7, with a button beneath the result reading “See Emaj7 in a progression →”.") }}
          <figcaption><b>Emaj7</b>One click from the ii–V–I in E.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>The CAGED map</h3>
        <p class="note">Standard guitar only</p>
        <p>The CAGED map shows how the five open shapes move across the neck. See how they overlap, or highlight one shape on its own. Change the root and watch the chord appear in all five positions. “Play the C chord in the A shape” will finally make sense.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-view-caged.html" %}
        {% include "musicprism/module-highlight.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          <div class="shot-cycle shot-cycle-6">
            {{ shot("caged", "Music Prism’s full-neck CAGED map for C major, showing the five open shapes bracketed across the fretboard, each highlighted in turn.") }}
            {% for s in ["C", "A", "G", "E", "D"] %}{{ frame("caged-" + s, loop.index) }}{% endfor %}
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>C major in all five shapes</b>One at a time, or all together.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>Progressions</h3>
        <p>Sometimes when you’re practicing you’re not playing a song, but you don’t want to just noodle around either. What you want is a <em>progression</em>: the chord sequences songs are built from.</p>
        <p>I–IV–V: blues, folk, and early rock and roll ran on little else for decades. iv–VII–III–VI: “Autumn Leaves” in a single breath, and a jazz-standard staple. Thirteen of them are built in, root position or voice-led. Learn a few and you’ll start hearing every song differently.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-view-progressions.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("progression-blues", "Music Prism laying out the twelve-bar blues in E major on guitar, as a grid of bars.") }}
          <figcaption><b>The 12-bar blues in E</b>Root position, on guitar.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("progression-doowop", "Music Prism laying out the I–vi–IV–V progression in G major on piano, each chord voice-led from the one before.") }}
          <figcaption><b>I–vi–IV–V in G</b>Voice-led, on piano.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>Scales</h3>
        <p>Where melody comes from, in three layers: the chord tells you where home is, the pentatonic adds the two notes that turn it into vocabulary, and the full scale fills in the colors.</p>
        <p>Rather than one huge chart that’s hard to read and harder to memorize, you add and subtract the layers yourself, so it stays clear what you’re actually looking at.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-view-scales.html" %}
        {% include "musicprism/module-show.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          <div class="shot-cycle shot-cycle-3">
            {{ shot("scales", "Music Prism showing the G major scale across the full guitar neck, built up in layers from the chord tones.") }}
            {{ frame("scales-triad", 1) }}{{ frame("scales-pent", 2) }}
          </div>
          <figcaption><b>G major across the neck</b>Built in layers: triad, pentatonic, scale.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="plate">
      <div class="plate-text">
        <h3>Musical keys and the Nashville Number System</h3>
        <p>When you’re ready, explore keys, and how chords become the numerals of the Nashville Number System (I, ii, iii, IV…), the same in every key. Change the key and every diagram follows, spelled the way that key spells it.</p>
        {% include "musicprism/module-key.html" %}
        {% include "musicprism/module-degrees.html" %}
      </div>
      <div class="plate-shots">
        <figure class="exhibit">
          {{ shot("keys-nashville", "Music Prism with the key set to E♭ minor: the key row, the degree row spelling VI as C♭, and every root chip carrying its roman numeral, above E♭ minor inversions on guitar.", 1216) }}
          <figcaption><b>E♭ minor</b>Every root shows its numeral: the VI chord is C♭.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>

