    </figure>
    <figure class="exhibit">
      <div class="mat"><img src="/assets/img/musicprism-printed-piano.webp" data-full="/assets/img/musicprism-printed-piano-full.webp" alt="The same Cmaj7 sheet printed for piano, showing four inversions in close position." width="900" height="1165" loading="lazy" decoding="async"></div>
      <figcaption><b>Cmaj7 on piano</b>The same sheet, for the keyboard.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="spelled" id="computed">
    <div class="copy">
      <h2>Is that chord <b>spelled</b> right?</h2>
      <p>Nothing here is a scanned chord chart. Every dot is placed by counting intervals from the root against the tuning of the instrument you picked, and every note is spelled the way theory says it should be.</p>
      <p>The third of F♯maj7 is A♯ and its seventh is E♯, because a maj7 stacks thirds, and the letters have to step F–A–C–E. Chord books and tab sites get this wrong routinely. It’s the kind of detail you don’t need until you do, and then you really do.</p>
      <p>Drawing from the math instead of from a library is also why the app can grow: a new instrument is just a new tuning.</p>
    </div>
    {% include "musicprism/atom.html" %}
  </div>

  <div class="coda" id="download">
